WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:03.203
[music playing]

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:05.372
ALBERT LIN: I'm headed
to the lost city

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:08.241
of Chan Chan, once
the beating heart

00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:11.144
of the mighty Chimu empire.

00:00:11.211 --> 00:00:12.045
Is that a pyramid?

00:00:12.112 --> 00:00:14.614
I think that's a pyramid.

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:17.617
A pyramid at Chan Chan.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:21.187
Can I find the answers
inside the city walls

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:23.256
as to why the
children had to die?

00:00:28.128 --> 00:00:31.531
Built over a thousand years
ago, amazingly much of the city

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:32.499
has survived.

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:39.439
And I've been told to expect
something spectacular.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.049
Look at these walls.

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:51.851
They stretch as far
as the eye can see.

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:02.228
I got my drone up, taking
a closer look right now.

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:06.433
Looks like a labyrinth of walls.

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:14.741
I'm using photogrammetry
data with graphic modeling

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:19.045
to rebuild the city and
these extraordinary walls.

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:24.784
Here they are restored to
their full glory, a truly awe

00:01:24.851 --> 00:01:27.754
inspiring sight rising
out of the desert

00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:31.724
like something out of a fable.

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:34.427
At its dazzling peak
in the 15th century,

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:39.032
the city was a bustling
home to 60,000 people,

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:41.868
about the size of
London at the time,

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:46.673
and covering 14 square miles.

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:51.344
This is one of the greatest
cities of human history.

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:54.347
So why did it
collapse so suddenly?

00:02:02.722 --> 00:02:05.391
I'm here to meet
Peruvian archaeologist,

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:10.396
Arturo Paredes Nunez, who's
been working here for years.

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:13.366
Ironically his last
name means wall.

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.742
[speaking spanish]

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:30.817
This is Chan Chan.

00:02:35.188 --> 00:02:36.556
Look at all these carvings here.

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:43.396
It's-- it looks
like digital fish.

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:47.634
The Chimu covered the city
with carvings of the ocean.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.002
Wow.

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:51.271
Look at these.

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:54.274
They're everywhere.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.076
But among all these
artworks is one Arturo

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:58.511
really thinks I need to see.

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:07.186
An entire wall in
the central palace

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:11.491
is devoted to a giant
mural over 170 feet long.

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:20.333
Wow.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.502
Wow.

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:23.469
Look at this.

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:27.173
What is this?

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.142
ARTURO PAREDES NUNEZ:
[speaking spanish]

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:29.742
ALBERT LIN: Fish.

00:03:29.809 --> 00:03:30.577
Yes.

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:31.778
[speaking spanish]

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:35.248
And each one of these
is actually a wave moving

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:37.917
down towards the coastline.

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:38.952
Are these currents?

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:41.287
[speaking spanish]

00:03:42.789 --> 00:03:45.325
ALBERT LIN: All these fish,
they're going in a direction.

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:48.328
They're traveling
from south to north.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:51.731
They're actually following
the course of a current,

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:54.334
the Peruvian current.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.670
But a closer look reveals that
the mural also depicts a moment

00:03:57.737 --> 00:04:01.040
of great change, another
current coming back

00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:06.145
in the other direction, fish
swimming in the opposite way,

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:09.182
north to south.

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:10.083
Incredible.

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:11.017
Is this right?

00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:12.118
Did I get this?

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:13.987
Did I get this right?

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:17.290
It looks like the Chimu are
recording a dramatic shift

00:04:17.357 --> 00:04:19.926
in the ocean currents.

00:04:19.993 --> 00:04:24.263
And when that happens, something
else dramatic happens, too.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.399
A violent shift in the climate.

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:34.173
Were the Chimu recording
a huge natural disaster

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:35.908
on those walls?

00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:38.244
Were they chiseling
a premonition

00:04:38.311 --> 00:04:40.179
of their own destruction?

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:48.187
I need to check the data
to see if they were right.

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:54.661
This is a dry, arid desert.

00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:57.463
But a sudden switch in
the Pacific Ocean current

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:04.203
can trigger violent change
and devastating rain.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.005
Look at this.

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:07.573
Everything flips.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.443
Because the ocean currents are
tied to the weather currents.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:13.046
Everything's tied together.

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:14.580
I know this kind of thing.

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:19.052
I live on the Pacific coast
myself, just in North America.

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:21.120
This climate shift
is called an El Nino.

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:30.797
These days, it happens
about once every five years.

00:05:33.433 --> 00:05:35.835
But once in a generation,
you get a really

00:05:35.902 --> 00:05:39.439
big or long lasting event.

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:41.174
That's called a Super El Nino.

00:05:44.343 --> 00:05:47.346
And the impact can
be catastrophic.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:51.984
Wait a minute.

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:54.087
Look at this, the data right
here, this rainfall data.

00:05:54.153 --> 00:05:59.125
Every year for the last decade,
less than half an inch of rain.

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:02.495
And then boom,
2017, one big spike,

00:06:02.562 --> 00:06:05.031
four inches of rain
in a single year

00:06:05.098 --> 00:06:06.999
completely out of the blue.

00:06:07.066 --> 00:06:12.472
It happened as recently as
2017, destructive floods in Peru

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:17.210
caused by torrential rains
triggered by a severe El Nino.

00:06:25.618 --> 00:06:28.788
Maybe there's clues in the
experiences in that El Nino

00:06:28.855 --> 00:06:34.227
that'll tell me what might
have happened to the Chimu.

00:06:34.293 --> 00:06:36.095
I'm gonna go try to
meet some people.

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:44.203
In 2017, the city of Trujillo,
just three miles from Chan Chan

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.340
and also sandwiched between
the sea and the mountains,

00:06:47.406 --> 00:06:49.275
was one of the worst hit.

00:06:49.342 --> 00:06:53.513
[dog barking]

00:07:05.658 --> 00:07:10.730
I'm here to meet some people
who almost lost everything.

00:07:10.797 --> 00:07:12.365
One local family
filmed the flood

00:07:12.431 --> 00:07:14.066
as it tore through their home.

00:07:25.211 --> 00:07:26.813
Never seen anything
like this before,

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:29.715
this much moving material,
water just rushing

00:07:29.782 --> 00:07:31.751
down through the streets.

00:07:31.818 --> 00:07:33.686
This whole thing would
have been a river.

00:07:33.753 --> 00:07:36.222
You can actually see the
storm in the background.

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:39.091
[inaudible]

00:07:39.158 --> 00:07:41.394
The more I talked to
people in Trujillo,

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.530
the more I hear this
word, [inaudible]..

00:07:44.597 --> 00:07:47.066
It doesn't sound
like Spanish to me.

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:49.168
[inaudible] is Quechua or is--

00:07:49.235 --> 00:07:50.102
Quechua.

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:51.671
It is?

00:07:51.737 --> 00:07:55.975
Quechua is an ancient language
of Peru, predating the Spanish.

00:07:56.042 --> 00:07:57.376
Wow, that's incredible.

00:07:57.443 --> 00:07:59.345
This has happened so
many times for so long

00:07:59.412 --> 00:08:00.746
that there's an ancient word.

00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:04.183
It's something that's
embedded in the memory.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.252
[inaudible]

00:08:06.319 --> 00:08:08.554
[speaking spanish]

00:08:08.621 --> 00:08:11.791
An avalanche.

00:08:11.858 --> 00:08:15.828
This term, [inaudible],, is
used to describe a combination

00:08:15.895 --> 00:08:18.497
of water, mud, stone, debris.

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:24.537
Coming not from the
ocean in the west,

00:08:24.604 --> 00:08:27.740
but from the east in the
mountains behind the city.

00:08:40.052 --> 00:08:42.455
Is it possible
that the same type

00:08:42.521 --> 00:08:46.325
of event happened to the Chimu?

00:08:46.392 --> 00:08:48.461
If it did, then there
must be some way

00:08:48.527 --> 00:08:51.397
of finding evidence
that an El Nino

00:08:51.464 --> 00:08:55.234
event happened in their time.

00:08:55.301 --> 00:09:00.139
And the answers may be
up in those mountains.

00:09:00.206 --> 00:09:03.576
[soft music]

